// battles and turning points. at: province (marker at its centre, nudged by dx/dy in map units).
// threshold: play slows down and holds here.
const EVENTS = [
  { id: 'kamigoryo', year: 1467, en: 'The Ōnin War begins', ja: '応仁の乱', at: 'yamashiro', kind: 'battle', threshold: true,
    sides: ['hatakeyama', 'hatakeyama'], winner: null,
    short: 'Two Hatakeyama heirs fight at Kamigoryō shrine in Kyoto, and the great houses take sides: the Eastern Army of Hosokawa Katsumoto against the Western Army of Yamana Sōzen.',
    long: 'The war had many causes: a shogun with two possible heirs, and succession quarrels in the Hatakeyama and Shiba houses, each claimant backed by either the Hosokawa or the Yamana. In February 1467 Hatakeyama Yoshinari attacked his cousin Masanaga at Kamigoryō shrine. By summer some 160,000 men were camped in Kyoto, the East around the shogun\'s palace, the West in the district still called Nishijin, "the western camp". For ten years they fought street by street, burning temples, palaces and whole districts. Both commanders died in 1473; the armies drifted home by 1477 with nothing decided. Kyoto lay in ruins, and the provincial governors who had gone to fight in the capital found their own deputies in charge at home. The age of the warring states had begun.' },
  { id: 'kaga', year: 1488, en: 'The Kaga league takes a province', ja: '加賀一向一揆', at: 'kaga', kind: 'event',
    short: 'The Ikkō league of Kaga besieges Takao castle and drives its governor, Togashi Masachika, to suicide.',
    long: 'The True Pure Land sect had spread through the farming villages of the Hokuriku under the preacher Rennyo. In 1488 its leagues, joined by local warriors, turned on the governor of Kaga, Togashi Masachika, who had used them to win his own war and then tried to crush them. Takao castle fell and Masachika killed himself. For almost a century Kaga was ruled by the leagues and the sect\'s temples, the most famous example of the period\'s "rule from below".' },
  { id: 'meio', year: 1493, en: 'The Meiō coup', ja: '明応の政変', at: 'yamashiro', kind: 'event',
    short: 'Hosokawa Masamoto deposes the shogun and installs his own candidate. From now on, shoguns are made by whoever holds Kyoto.',
    long: 'While Shogun Ashikaga Yoshiki (later Yoshitane) was away on campaign in Kawachi with Hatakeyama Masanaga, Hosokawa Masamoto seized Kyoto and set up a different Ashikaga as shogun. Masanaga was killed; Yoshiki fled and spent years trying to return. Historians often date the start of the Sengoku period from this coup, since the shogun was now openly a pawn.' },
  { id: 'izu', year: 1493, en: 'Hōjō Sōun takes Izu', ja: '伊豆討ち入り', at: 'izu', kind: 'event',
    short: 'Ise Shinkurō, later called Hōjō Sōun, crosses into Izu and overthrows the Horigoe kubō, the Ashikaga\'s governor of the east. Two years later he takes Odawara.',
    long: 'Ise Shinkurō came east as a relative and envoy of the Imagawa, with a castle of his own in Suruga. In 1493, as the Ashikaga were split by the Meiō coup, he attacked the Horigoe kubō, Ashikaga Chachamaru, and took Izu. Around 1495 he seized Odawara castle in Sagami, by legend through a trick during a hunt. His family would rule from Odawara for five generations, and he is often called the first true Sengoku daimyo.' },
  { id: 'kawagoe', year: 1546, en: 'Night battle of Kawagoe', ja: '河越夜戦', at: 'musashi', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['hojo', 'uesugi'], winner: 'hojo',
    short: 'A small Hōjō army breaks a huge Uesugi coalition besieging Kawagoe castle with a night attack, and the Hōjō become masters of the Kantō.',
    long: 'The two Uesugi lines and the Koga kubō, briefly united, besieged Kawagoe castle in Musashi, held by Hōjō Tsunashige. Hōjō Ujiyasu came to relieve it with a much smaller force, made a show of asking for terms, then attacked at night. The coalition broke: Uesugi Tomosada of the Ōgigayatsu line was killed and his line ended, and Uesugi Norimasa fled. Within a few years he had abandoned the Kantō for Echigo.' },
  { id: 'taineiji', year: 1551, en: 'The fall of Ōuchi Yoshitaka', ja: '大寧寺の変', at: 'nagato', kind: 'event',
    short: 'Sue Harukata, the Ōuchi\'s leading general, rises against his lord. Yoshitaka flees and dies at Tainei-ji temple. The richest house of the west falls from within.',
    long: 'Ōuchi Yoshitaka had turned from war to culture after a failed invasion of Izumo in 1543, and his military men resented his courtiers. In 1551 Sue Harukata seized Yamaguchi. Yoshitaka fled to the temple of Tainei-ji in Nagato and took his own life. Sue installed a puppet, Ōuchi Yoshinaga, brother of Ōtomo Sōrin, and ruled in his name, until Mōri Motonari, once an Ōuchi vassal, turned on him.' },
  { id: 'itsukushima', year: 1555, en: 'Battle of Itsukushima', ja: '厳島の戦い', at: 'aki', dx: 6, dy: 8, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['mori', 'ouchi'], winner: 'mori',
    short: 'Mōri Motonari lures Sue Harukata\'s much larger army onto the sacred island of Itsukushima and destroys it in a dawn attack in a storm.',
    long: 'Motonari built a small fort on Itsukushima as bait and let rumours spread that he feared it would be attacked. Sue landed some 20,000 men on the island. On a stormy night Motonari crossed with about 4,000; at dawn his force struck from the hills behind the Sue camp while the fleets of the Murakami sea lords cut off the escape. Sue Harukata died on the island. Within two years the Mōri held all the old Ōuchi provinces of Suō and Nagato.' },
  { id: 'okehazama', year: 1560, en: 'Battle of Okehazama', ja: '桶狭間の戦い', at: 'owari', dx: 6, dy: 5, kind: 'battle', threshold: true,
    sides: ['oda', 'imagawa'], winner: 'oda',
    short: 'Oda Nobunaga, 25 and outnumbered by perhaps ten to one, surprises Imagawa Yoshimoto\'s army resting in a narrow valley during a storm, and kills him.',
    long: 'Imagawa Yoshimoto, lord of three provinces, marched west into Owari in June 1560, perhaps aiming at Kyoto, perhaps only at the Oda border forts. His vanguard, led by the young Matsudaira Motoyasu, the future Ieyasu, took the fort of Marune. Yoshimoto halted to rest in the gorge of Okehazama. Nobunaga, with two or three thousand men, approached under cover of a sudden storm and charged the Imagawa headquarters. Yoshimoto was killed. The battle made Nobunaga\'s name, freed the Matsudaira from the Imagawa, and began the Imagawa collapse.' },
  { id: 'kawanakajima', year: 1561, en: 'Fourth battle of Kawanakajima', ja: '第四次川中島の戦い', at: 'shinano', dx: 2, dy: -14, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['takeda', 'uesugi'], winner: null,
    short: 'The bloodiest of five meetings between Takeda Shingen and Uesugi Kenshin on the plain of Kawanakajima in northern Shinano. Both sides lost thousands, and neither won.',
    long: 'Takeda Shingen planned to split his army and trap the Uesugi on Saijo hill; Kenshin, guessing the plan from the smoke of the Takeda cooking fires, came down in the night and fell on Shingen\'s main body in the morning fog. Legend has Kenshin riding into the Takeda headquarters and striking at Shingen, who parried with his war fan. The Takeda flanking force arrived in time to drive the Uesugi off. Shingen\'s brother Nobushige and his strategist Yamamoto Kansuke were among the dead. Northern Shinano stayed Takeda.' },
  { id: 'konodai', year: 1564, en: 'Second battle of Kōnodai', ja: '第二次国府台合戦', at: 'shimosa', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['hojo', 'satomi'], winner: 'hojo',
    short: 'The Hōjō defeat the Satomi on the heights of Kōnodai, by the bay, and take control of Shimōsa and much of Kazusa.',
    long: 'Satomi Yoshihiro crossed from the Bōsō peninsula in support of the anti-Hōjō lords of the Kantō and camped at Kōnodai. Hōjō Ujiyasu and Ujimasa attacked across the Edo river; after an initial setback they surprised the Satomi army and routed it. The Chiba of Shimōsa became Hōjō vassals, and the Satomi were pushed back south.' },
  { id: 'gassantoda', year: 1566, en: 'Fall of Gassan-Toda', ja: '月山富田城の戦い', at: 'izumo', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['mori', 'amago'], winner: 'mori',
    short: 'After a long siege, Amago Yoshihisa surrenders the mountain fortress of Gassan-Toda to Mōri Motonari. The Amago realm ends.',
    long: 'Mōri Motonari invaded Izumo in 1562 and slowly isolated Gassan-Toda, one of the strongest castles in Japan. Rather than storm it, he cut its supplies and offered pardons to anyone who came out, so that the garrison dwindled. In 1566 Amago Yoshihisa surrendered. The Mōri now held most of western Honshū.' },
  { id: 'inabayama', year: 1567, en: 'Nobunaga takes Mino', ja: '稲葉山城の戦い', at: 'mino', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['oda', 'saito'], winner: 'oda',
    short: 'After years of attacks, Nobunaga takes Inabayama castle from Saitō Tatsuoki, renames it Gifu, and adopts a seal reading "rule the realm by force".',
    long: 'Nobunaga had been at war with Mino since his father-in-law Saitō Dōsan was killed by his own son in 1556. In 1567, with several of the "Mino Triumvirate" of senior Saitō vassals going over to him, he took Inabayama. He renamed castle and town Gifu and began to use the seal tenka fubu, "the realm under military rule", a statement of where he meant to go next.' },
  { id: 'kyoto1568', year: 1568, en: 'Nobunaga enters Kyoto', ja: '織田信長の上洛', at: 'yamashiro', kind: 'event',
    short: 'Nobunaga marches on the capital with Ashikaga Yoshiaki, sweeps aside the Rokkaku and the Miyoshi, and makes Yoshiaki shogun.',
    long: 'Ashikaga Yoshiaki, brother of the murdered shogun Yoshiteru, had been looking for a protector. Nobunaga took him up, marched through Ōmi, took the Rokkaku castle of Kannonji, and entered Kyoto in October 1568. The Miyoshi armies withdrew. Yoshiaki became the fifteenth shogun, and Nobunaga, declining most titles, became the real power in the capital.' },
  { id: 'anegawa', year: 1570, en: 'Battle of Anegawa', ja: '姉川の戦い', at: 'omi', dx: 2, dy: -10, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['oda', 'azai'], winner: 'oda',
    short: 'Oda and Tokugawa beat the Azai and Asakura on the banks of the Ane river, but cannot yet finish them.',
    long: 'When Nobunaga invaded Echizen in the spring of 1570, his brother-in-law Azai Nagamasa sided with the Asakura and nearly trapped him. That summer Nobunaga returned with Tokugawa Ieyasu. The armies met at the Ane river: Tokugawa against the Asakura, Oda against the Azai. After hard fighting, a Tokugawa flank attack broke the Asakura and the Azai followed. The Azai and Asakura survived three more years.' },
  { id: 'honganji', year: 1570, en: 'The Ishiyama Hongan-ji war begins', ja: '石山合戦', at: 'settsu', kind: 'event',
    short: 'The fortress-temple of the True Pure Land sect in Osaka rises against Nobunaga. It holds out for ten years.',
    long: 'Ishiyama Hongan-ji, at the mouth of the Yodo river where Osaka castle now stands, was the headquarters of the True Pure Land sect and its leagues. In 1570 the abbot Kennyo called on the faithful to resist Nobunaga. Supplied by sea by the Mōri, whose fleet beat the Oda navy in 1576, the temple held out until 1580, when Kennyo accepted the emperor\'s mediation and left. The buildings burned.' },
  { id: 'hiei', year: 1571, en: 'Mount Hiei burned', ja: '比叡山焼き討ち', at: 'omi', dx: -8, dy: 8, kind: 'event',
    short: 'Nobunaga burns the great monastery of Enryaku-ji on Mount Hiei, which had sheltered his enemies, killing thousands of monks and lay people.',
    long: 'Enryaku-ji, above Kyoto, had for centuries been a religious and military power with its own warrior monks. It had sheltered the Azai and Asakura armies. In September 1571 Nobunaga surrounded the mountain and burned its temples and the town at its foot; contemporary sources speak of thousands killed, though excavations suggest the destruction may have been smaller than they claim. The act shocked the country and showed that Nobunaga would recognise no authority beyond his own.' },
  { id: 'mikatagahara', year: 1573, en: 'Battle of Mikatagahara', ja: '三方ヶ原の戦い', at: 'totomi', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['takeda', 'tokugawa'], winner: 'takeda',
    short: 'Takeda Shingen crushes Tokugawa Ieyasu on a plateau north of Hamamatsu. It is Ieyasu\'s worst defeat; Shingen dies a few months later.',
    long: 'Shingen marched into Tōtōmi in late 1572 with perhaps 27,000 men, apparently heading for Kyoto. Ieyasu, with some 11,000 Tokugawa and Oda troops, came out of Hamamatsu castle on 25 January 1573 (the twelfth month of Genki 3) and was routed on the Mikatagahara plateau. He escaped back to the castle and, by legend, had its gates left open and fires lit, and the Takeda did not attack. Ieyasu is said to have had his portrait painted in defeat as a reminder. Shingen fell ill and died that spring; the advance west ended.' },
  { id: 'end_muromachi', year: 1573, en: 'The last shogun is driven out', ja: '室町幕府の滅亡', at: 'yamashiro', dx: -6, dy: 6, kind: 'event',
    short: 'Ashikaga Yoshiaki rises against Nobunaga, is defeated and expelled from Kyoto. The Muromachi shogunate ends after 235 years. Later that year the Azai and Asakura are destroyed.',
    long: 'Yoshiaki had organised much of the coalition against his protector. With Shingen dead, Nobunaga besieged him at Makishima castle and sent him into exile in 1573. Yoshiaki kept the title of shogun for years, under Mōri protection, but the shogunate was over. In August and September Nobunaga destroyed the Asakura, burning Ichijōdani, and took Odani from the Azai.' },
  { id: 'nagashino', year: 1575, en: 'Battle of Nagashino', ja: '長篠の戦い', at: 'mikawa', dx: 6, dy: -6, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['oda', 'takeda'], winner: 'oda',
    short: 'Oda and Tokugawa guns, firing from behind wooden palisades, destroy Takeda Katsuyori\'s army as it attacks across open ground. Many of the Takeda\'s senior generals die.',
    long: 'Takeda Katsuyori besieged the Tokugawa castle of Nagashino in Mikawa. Nobunaga and Ieyasu came to its relief with some 38,000 men and placed perhaps 3,000 arquebusiers behind a line of wooden fences across the plain of Shitaragahara. Katsuyori attacked. The Takeda charges were shot down, and the Oda and Tokugawa counter-attacked. How many guns, and whether they fired in rotating volleys, is still debated, but the result is not: the Takeda lost much of their leadership, and never recovered.' },
  { id: 'tedorigawa', year: 1577, en: 'Battle of Tedorigawa', ja: '手取川の戦い', at: 'kaga', dx: -4, dy: 4, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['uesugi', 'oda'], winner: 'uesugi',
    short: 'Uesugi Kenshin catches Shibata Katsuie\'s Oda army at the Tedori river in Kaga; the Oda lose about a thousand men. It is Kenshin\'s last battle: he dies the next spring.',
    long: 'Having taken Nanao castle in Noto, Kenshin moved into Kaga against the Oda army under Shibata Katsuie, which had come north too late to save Nanao. Accounts differ on how it went: in one, Kenshin lured the Oda into a frontal attack across the river; in another, he caught them withdrawing across it at night. Either way the Oda lost about a thousand men and fell back south. Kenshin\'s victory raised fears of a march on Kyoto, but he died in April 1578, and his adopted sons fought each other for the succession.' },
  { id: 'mimigawa', year: 1578, en: 'Battle of Mimigawa', ja: '耳川の戦い', at: 'hyuga', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['shimazu', 'otomo'], winner: 'shimazu',
    short: 'The Shimazu destroy the great Ōtomo army sent into Hyūga, and northern Kyushu begins to fall away from the Ōtomo.',
    long: 'Ōtomo Sōrin, newly baptised, sent a large army south to restore the Itō of Hyūga, whom the Shimazu had driven out. It besieged Takajō castle, and the Shimazu came to its relief. Using their favourite tactic, a feigned retreat that drew the enemy into ambush, the Shimazu broke the Ōtomo army on the Mimi river. Thousands drowned or were cut down. The Ōtomo\'s vassals began to change sides.' },
  { id: 'tottori', year: 1581, en: 'Siege of Tottori', ja: '鳥取城の戦い', at: 'inaba', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['oda', 'mori'], winner: 'oda',
    short: 'Hideyoshi surrounds Tottori castle after buying up the region\'s rice, and starves the Mōri garrison into surrender.',
    long: 'Before the siege, Hideyoshi had merchants buy rice in Inaba at high prices, so the castle had little in store. He then built a ring of forts around Tottori and waited. After months of siege (accounts give up to 200 days) the garrison was starving; its commander, Kikkawa Tsuneie, killed himself in exchange for the lives of his men. The "starvation siege" became one of the period\'s grimmest stories.' },
  { id: 'tenmokuzan', year: 1582, en: 'The end of the Takeda', ja: '武田氏滅亡', at: 'kai', kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['oda', 'takeda'], winner: 'oda',
    short: 'Oda and Tokugawa armies invade the Takeda lands. Vassals defect, castles fall, and Takeda Katsuyori dies with his family at Tenmokuzan.',
    long: 'In early 1582 a Takeda vassal in Kiso went over to the Oda, and Nobunaga\'s son Nobutada led armies into Shinano while Ieyasu came up from Suruga and the Hōjō from the east. The Takeda realm collapsed in about a month. Katsuyori, refused shelter by his own retainer Oyamada Nobushige, killed himself with his wife near Tenmokuzan. Nobunaga divided the Takeda lands among his generals.' },
  { id: 'honnoji', year: 1582, en: 'The Honnō-ji incident', ja: '本能寺の変', at: 'yamashiro', dx: 4, dy: -4, kind: 'battle', threshold: true,
    sides: ['oda', 'oda'], winner: null,
    short: 'At dawn on 21 June 1582, Nobunaga\'s general Akechi Mitsuhide surrounds the temple of Honnō-ji in Kyoto, where Nobunaga is staying with a small guard. Nobunaga dies in the burning temple.',
    long: 'Nobunaga was on his way west to support Hideyoshi against the Mōri. Akechi Mitsuhide, ordered to march west too, turned his army toward Kyoto instead and attacked Honnō-ji. Nobunaga fought, then withdrew into the burning building and died; his heir Nobutada was killed at Nijō the same day. Why Mitsuhide did it is one of Japanese history\'s great mysteries: revenge, ambition, fear for his own future, or a plot by others. He held power for thirteen days.' },
  { id: 'yamazaki', year: 1582, en: 'Battle of Yamazaki', ja: '山崎の戦い', at: 'yamashiro', dx: -8, dy: 2, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['toyotomi', 'oda'], winner: 'toyotomi',
    short: 'Hideyoshi makes peace with the Mōri, races his army back east in days, and destroys Akechi Mitsuhide at Yamazaki. Mitsuhide is killed fleeing.',
    long: 'Hideyoshi was besieging the Mōri castle of Takamatsu in Bitchū by flooding it when news of Honnō-ji reached him. He hid it from the Mōri, agreed terms within a day, and marched his army some 200 km back in about a week, the "great return from Chūgoku". At Yamazaki, between the mountains and the river south of Kyoto, he beat Mitsuhide\'s smaller army; Mitsuhide was killed by peasants as he fled. As Nobunaga\'s avenger, Hideyoshi took the lead among his generals.' },
  { id: 'shizugatake', year: 1583, en: 'Battle of Shizugatake', ja: '賤ヶ岳の戦い', at: 'omi', dx: 4, dy: -16, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['toyotomi', 'oda'], winner: 'toyotomi',
    short: 'Hideyoshi defeats Shibata Katsuie in the mountains of northern Ōmi and becomes Nobunaga\'s real successor. Katsuie dies at his castle with his wife Oichi.',
    long: 'Nobunaga\'s senior general Shibata Katsuie, allied with Nobunaga\'s son Nobutaka, opposed Hideyoshi\'s rise. In spring 1583 their armies faced each other near Lake Yogo. When Katsuie\'s nephew Sakuma Morimasa pushed too far forward, Hideyoshi force-marched his army back in a single night and broke him. Maeda Toshiie withdrew from Katsuie\'s side. Hideyoshi pursued Katsuie to Kitanoshō, where Katsuie and Oichi killed themselves. Hideyoshi began building Osaka castle that year.' },
  { id: 'komaki', year: 1584, en: 'Komaki and Nagakute', ja: '小牧・長久手の戦い', at: 'owari', dx: -4, dy: -6, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['tokugawa', 'toyotomi'], winner: null,
    short: 'Tokugawa Ieyasu and Oda Nobukatsu face Hideyoshi for months. Ieyasu wins the one big clash, at Nagakute, but the campaign ends in a negotiated peace.',
    long: 'Nobunaga\'s son Nobukatsu turned to Ieyasu against Hideyoshi. The armies dug in near Komaki in Owari. A Hideyoshi force tried to strike at Ieyasu\'s home province of Mikawa and was caught and destroyed at Nagakute, with the deaths of Ikeda Tsuneoki and Mori Nagayoshi. Hideyoshi then made a separate peace with Nobukatsu, leaving Ieyasu without a cause; Ieyasu sent a son as hostage, and in 1586 came to Osaka to submit. He had shown he could not be beaten, and Hideyoshi treated him accordingly.' },
  { id: 'okitanawate', year: 1584, en: 'Battle of Okitanawate', ja: '沖田畷の戦い', at: 'hizen', dx: 8, dy: 14, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['shimazu', 'ryuzoji'], winner: 'shimazu',
    short: 'Shimazu Iehisa and Arima Harunobu trap Ryūzōji Takanobu\'s far larger army on narrow paths through the marshes of Shimabara. Takanobu is killed.',
    long: 'Ryūzōji Takanobu marched on the Arima of Shimabara, who had called on the Shimazu for help. Shimazu Iehisa, with Portuguese guns and a Portuguese ship supplied through the Jesuit mission, placed his small army where the road narrowed between marsh and sea, then used a feigned retreat to draw the Ryūzōji forward. Trapped on the causeways and shot at from the flanks, the Ryūzōji army broke, and Takanobu was killed in his palanquin. The Shimazu were now the strongest power in Kyushu.' },
  { id: 'shikoku', year: 1585, en: 'The conquest of Shikoku', ja: '四国征伐', at: 'awa_shikoku', kind: 'event',
    short: 'Hideyoshi sends over 100,000 men into Shikoku. Chōsokabe Motochika, who had just united the island, surrenders and keeps only Tosa.',
    long: 'With central Japan secure, Hideyoshi turned on Shikoku. His half-brother Hidenaga landed in Awa, Ukita Hideie in Sanuki, and the Mōri under Kobayakawa Takakage in Iyo. Chōsokabe Motochika, facing armies far bigger than his, surrendered within weeks. The same year Hideyoshi became regent (kampaku), and the next he took the new surname Toyotomi.' },
  { id: 'kyushu', year: 1587, en: 'The conquest of Kyushu', ja: '九州平定', at: 'satsuma', dx: 4, dy: -16, kind: 'event',
    short: 'Hideyoshi leads a vast army into Kyushu at the Ōtomo\'s request. The Shimazu, who had taken almost the whole island, surrender and are cut back to Satsuma and Ōsumi.',
    long: 'The Shimazu ignored Hideyoshi\'s orders to stop their war on the Ōtomo, and defeated an advance army at Hetsugigawa in Bungo in early 1587. Hideyoshi then came himself, with perhaps 200,000 men in two columns through Higo and Hyūga. The Shimazu fell back and Shimazu Yoshihisa surrendered in Satsuma, shaving his head as a monk. In the same year Hideyoshi ordered the Jesuit missionaries to leave the country.' },
  { id: 'suriagehara', year: 1589, en: 'Battle of Suriagehara', ja: '摺上原の戦い', at: 'mutsu', dx: -14, dy: 40, kind: 'battle',
    sides: ['date', null], winner: 'date',
    short: 'Date Masamune destroys the Ashina at Suriagehara and takes Aizu, becoming master of southern Mutsu, just before Hideyoshi reaches the north.',
    long: 'Masamune, twenty-two, defied Hideyoshi\'s order to keep the peace and attacked the Ashina of Aizu. At Suriagehara, below Mount Bandai, the Date army broke the Ashina, whose lord fled. Masamune moved his seat to Kurokawa (Aizu). The following year Hideyoshi took most of his gains away.' },
  { id: 'odawara', year: 1590, en: 'The siege of Odawara', ja: '小田原征伐', at: 'sagami', kind: 'battle', threshold: true,
    sides: ['toyotomi', 'hojo'], winner: 'toyotomi',
    short: 'Hideyoshi besieges the Hōjō capital with over 200,000 men. Odawara surrenders after three months. The whole country now obeys one ruler.',
    long: 'The Hōjō refused to come to Kyoto and submit. Hideyoshi gathered armies from all over Japan, took the Hōjō\'s outlying castles one by one, and surrounded Odawara, building a castle of his own on a nearby hill in eighty days (the "overnight castle"). The siege was famously leisurely, with tea ceremonies and entertainers in Hideyoshi\'s camp. In August Hōjō Ujinao surrendered; his father Ujimasa was ordered to commit suicide. The northern lords came to submit, Date Masamune among them. Ieyasu received the Hōjō lands and moved to Edo.' },
  { id: 'korea', year: 1592, en: 'The invasions of Korea', ja: '文禄・慶長の役', at: 'tsushima', kind: 'event',
    short: 'Hideyoshi sends armies across the strait to invade Korea, aiming at Ming China. The war devastates Korea and ends with his death in 1598.',
    long: 'In 1592 some 150,000 men crossed from Kyushu by way of Tsushima and took Seoul within three weeks. Korean resistance on land, the Korean navy under Admiral Yi Sun-sin, and Ming Chinese armies stopped the advance. Peace talks failed, and a second invasion in 1597 got no further. After Hideyoshi\'s death in 1598 the armies were withdrawn. The war caused enormous suffering in Korea; in Japan it exhausted the western lords and divided Hideyoshi\'s generals.' },
  { id: 'hideyoshi_dies', year: 1598, en: 'Hideyoshi dies', ja: '秀吉の死', at: 'yamashiro', dx: 6, dy: 6, kind: 'event',
    short: 'Toyotomi Hideyoshi dies at Fushimi, leaving his five-year-old son Hideyori in the care of five elders and five commissioners. Tokugawa Ieyasu is the strongest of them.',
    long: 'Hideyoshi spent his last months making his great lords swear to protect Hideyori. The Five Elders (Tokugawa, Maeda, Mōri, Ukita, Uesugi) were to govern together with the Five Commissioners, administrators led by Ishida Mitsunari. Maeda Toshiie, the one elder who could restrain Ieyasu, died in 1599, and Ieyasu began arranging marriages and alliances in defiance of Hideyoshi\'s rules.' },
  { id: 'sekigahara', year: 1600, en: 'Battle of Sekigahara', ja: '関ヶ原の戦い', at: 'mino', dx: -8, dy: 4, kind: 'battle', threshold: true,
    sides: ['east', 'west'], winner: 'east',
    short: 'On 21 October 1600, in a foggy valley in Mino, the Eastern Army of Tokugawa Ieyasu meets Ishida Mitsunari\'s Western Army. When Kobayakawa Hideaki turns on his own side, the West collapses.',
    long: 'Some 160,000 men met at the crossroads of Sekigahara. The battle opened in fog in the morning and was evenly fought for hours. Many Western lords never moved: the Mōri and Kikkawa on the hills behind the East had already made terms. Around noon Kobayakawa Hideaki, after Ieyasu\'s guns fired toward his position, sent his army down against Ōtani Yoshitsugu, and other Western units followed his lead. The Western Army broke; the Shimazu escaped by charging through the Eastern lines. Ishida Mitsunari, Konishi Yukinaga and Ankokuji Ekei were executed in Kyoto. Ieyasu redistributed the domains of the losers, and the lands of Japan were now his to assign.' },
  { id: 'shogun', year: 1603, en: 'Ieyasu becomes shogun', ja: '江戸幕府の成立', at: 'musashi', dx: 6, dy: 4, kind: 'event',
    short: 'The emperor names Tokugawa Ieyasu shogun. His government at Edo will rule Japan for 265 years.',
    long: 'Ieyasu took the title the Ashikaga had held, and governed from Edo. Two years later he passed it to his son Hidetada, to show that it was hereditary, while keeping real power. Toyotomi Hideyori remained in Osaka castle as a rival until the Tokugawa besieged and destroyed it in 1614–15. The Sengoku period was over.' },
];

